## Scope and evidence

This review uses the export dated 4 October 2026, not the desk's current queue.
Report IDs count reports, not customers, bookings, or independent incidents.
The source ticket's prose says one known bug; its components and source data do not support that claim.
Read the report bodies, tenant directory, classifier, and queue timestamps together.

<IncidentReview />

## Parser failure

The shared parser reads month first for tenants whose locale writes day first.
For example, R-41200 from Harbour Dental (`en-GB`, `booking-v2`, app) describes
7 April becoming 4 July. R-41205 belongs to Morrow Tax (`fr-FR`), even though
its browser locale is `en-US` and intake calls it `hint`: the tenant's configuration matters.

Reproduce by passing `07/04/2026` as a day-first tenant's date through the shared
app/email parser. Expected: 7 April. Reported: 4 July. The export describes this behavior;
it does not contain the product executable, so this is a proposed reproduction, not a test we ran.
Make parsing follow an explicit tenant date contract. Check ambiguous dates, unambiguous
day-first dates, month-first tenants, and locale switching before closing the ticket.

## Form hint failure

`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` but reads the form in a day-first tenant's order.
This is not the same failure as the shared parser. Copper Studio, Alder Physio, and
Pale Harbour use that form. Their app/email reports still follow the parser path.
R-41230 is a Copper Studio form report tagged `parser` by intake, but the tenant/form
combination places it in the hint group.

Reproduce in a day-first `booking-v3` form: follow the month-first hint and enter
`01/06/2026` intending 6 January. Expected: 6 January. With day-first reading: 1 June.
Make the visible hint and validation agree with the tenant's date contract; test the form
separately from the app/email parser. A parser-only fix cannot establish that the hint is fixed.

## Unresolved observations are not a proven third cause

The desk notes identify reports that the classifier files as parser but whose bodies do not
demonstrate a month/day swap. One describes different days for Lisbon and Toronto;
another says an invite arrived before the booking. Keep these in a separate investigation
ticket rather than calling them confirmed parser failures or declaring a timezone bug proved.

Obtain the original entered date, event identifier, stored instant, timezone, all-day flag,
invite payload, and creation/delivery timestamps. Compare local-date preservation and
UTC conversion, including DST boundaries, without assuming they explain every report.
R-41236 needs a separate chronology check within this investigation. Split it further if evidence
establishes independent mechanisms. Reassign each report once, preserving its ID and audit trail.

## Replace subject-only triage

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) remains an historical record;
this proposed review does not silently edit or declare it superseded.
Its subject-only rule can merge different failures and separate duplicates of the same failure.
The executable `sameCause` function instead checks tenant locale, form, and channel.
Even that rule needs a body-level exception for contradictory observations.

Proposed triage: deduplicate export rows by report ID, inspect the body and tenant's configuration,
and group by supported failure mechanism. Keep uncertain reports in investigation. Similar
subjects, browser locales, or intake tags alone are not sufficient evidence for merging.
Seek desk approval for a new decision record before replacing the accepted operational policy.

## The queue did not stop when the desk marked the ticket known

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart computes reports received and reports merged separately from their timestamps.
The July merge batch is not the start of the failures: reports had already arrived in April.
Reports continue through the export's final date. A queued fix and a known status do not prove
resolution. R-41620 says its author stopped reporting; report volume alone cannot measure recovery.
The export cannot establish what happened after its snapshot. Read fresh desk data before
closing or treating these counts as current. Keep the source data unchanged so review remains auditable.

## Follow-up and verification

The [public review repo](https://github.com/karol-kacki3/mega-s01e03-support-kb) contains
one ticket per finding, disjoint report lists, and a testable report-counting routine.
Run `node --test scripts/incident-review.test.mjs` from its root to check the classification,
duplicate handling, and anomaly separation. Run `node scripts/prepare-tickets.mjs` to regenerate
the local ticket drafts from the source snapshot. Counts on this page belong in its component,
not handwritten totals that can drift. A new export requires a new review of the anomaly list too.

